    "accordion_faq_eter": {
      "type": "accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Full or half eternity: which should I choose?",
            "content": "<p>Half, for most people. A half eternity ring has stones across the top and a plain back, so it can be resized, sits flat beside a wedding ring and keeps the stones away from knocks. A full eternity ring is set all the way round, sparkles from every angle and usually cannot be resized, so it suits a finger whose size is settled.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What does an eternity ring mean, and when is it given?",
            "content": "<p>An eternity ring marks a milestone in a marriage or long relationship: traditionally a significant anniversary or the birth of a first child, and today any moment that matters. The unbroken line of stones is the symbol. There is no rule about who gives it or when; some couples choose one together.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Which finger is an eternity ring worn on?",
            "content": "<p>Most wear it on the left ring finger, stacked with the wedding ring nearest the hand, the engagement ring next and the eternity ring on top. Others wear it alone on the right hand. If it will sit against other rings, tell us which, so we size and shape it to fit beside them.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can an eternity ring be resized?",
            "content": "<p>A half eternity ring can, at the plain back of the band, and every ring includes one free resize within 12 months of delivery. A full eternity ring usually cannot, because there is no plain metal to work on. That is why we post a free ring sizer and confirm the size before a full eternity ring is made.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Which setting is most secure for daily wear?",
            "content": "<p>Channel and bar settings hold the stones between walls of metal and are the most protective. Claw settings show the most light and benefit from a regular check of the claws, which we do free of charge for life. Fine claw and grain settings hold small stones in a slim line and suit narrow rings.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What carat weight should an eternity ring be?",
            "content": "<p>The carat figure is the total of every stone. A half eternity ring of 0.25ct to 0.50ct reads as a fine, wearable band; 1ct and above is a statement. Keep the stones smaller than the centre stone of an engagement ring it sits beside, so the two do not compete. Every listing shows total weight and stone size.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "accor_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Diamonds or coloured stones?",
            "content": "<p>Sapphire and ruby are hard enough for daily wear in any setting. Emerald is softer and does best in a channel or bar setting on a half eternity ring, where the metal protects its edges. Diamonds, natural or lab-grown, suit every setting, and we also make mixed rows of diamonds and colour.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
